## Contract and scope

Reviewed the accepted vector-compatibility-open-policy ADR, approved narrow
requirements/design/plan at owner seq-359, relevant repository instructions and
memory, both implementation closure records, and integrated source against the
baseline. The implementation preserves the approved persistent-cache policy and
does not add a process cache, runtime descriptor, precision freeze, new provider
grammar, mean-recovery reorder, diagnostic CLI or dense-rebuild repair.

## Finding and resolution

The preliminary review found one P2 in immutable core commit `88669732c`:
the new explicit-None stored-profile preread converted malformed name/revision/
dimension decoding errors into generic I/O before the existing corruption
validator could classify them. SQLite's non-STRICT profile table permits BLOB
names/revisions and a negative dimension. This lost the existing
`EmbedderIdentityDrift`, stage, table-row locator and recovery hint.

Resolved by original RED `40b5a92f2` / GREEN `d04e3e597`, integrated as
`5110aa4af` / `416d1e54a`. The three real-SQLite regression tests assert the
original structured classification, locator and recovery fields. The corrected
decoder at `src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/open.rs:1234` distinguishes typed
row-decoding failures from SQLite execution errors. The final candidate retains
this fix. No stronger profile validator was requested or introduced.

## Full implementation assessment

- Production `EmbedderChoice` exposes only Default/None (`open.rs:2064`).
  Injection uses separately gated `TestEmbedderChoice` and explicit
  test/evaluation openers. Support-feature unification does not restore caller
  variants to normal admission. Low-level traits, standalone CLS, Noop,
  Nomic/ONNX and existing evaluation seams retain their scoped roles.
- New string projection selectors are rejected before projection mutation.
  Historical selectors remain truthful under None and cannot silently activate
  the attached provider. Explicit None uses stored identity metadata while
  retaining corruption rejection and existing mean-recovery behavior.
- `OpenPostProbeGuard` owns database resources before verification
  (`open.rs:902`, `:932`). Existing dispatcher cleanup and the 30-second
  production budget remain unchanged. Test-only observation moves before early
  rejection; separate early-timeout and later-startup-fault tests retain
  database-owner, admission-lock and retained-provider assertions.
- Registered references are confirmed before atomic publication
  (`vector_equivalence.rs:193`, `:205`, `:250`). Rejected newly collected
  references cannot seed subsequent admission. Existing references and
  registered/prospective marker-error differences are preserved. This does not
  claim whole-open rollback.
- Independently compared `hash_fingerprint_field`,
  `probe_verification_fingerprint`, `probe_verification_is_cached`,
  `record_probe_verification` and `clear_probe_verification` against baseline:
  all five bodies are byte-identical in the integrated implementation. Probe
  gate, numeric thresholds, structural ordering, marker authority/error rules,
  ordinary 90/45/0 cadence and persisted-marker restart reuse remain intact.
  The accepted same-identity backend-drift residual remains explicit.
- Numeric mismatch, typed verification causes and storage failures remain
  distinct. SDK, Python, NAPI/TypeScript and CLI mappings preserve original
  provider context/payloads and source/cause chains. Initial subscribers receive
  actual checker-refusal reporting; callback panic cannot replace that error.
- Obsolete refused-handle reports, methods and readiness modes are removed.
  Independent graph/structural deferred vocabulary and actual pending/deferred
  work semantics remain. The accidentally proposed graph removals were reverted
  and are not counted as required RED evidence.
- Interface documents and ADR supersession describe admission, cached
  verification limits and deliberate mutable None access. The known
  provider-free dense-rebuild invalidation defect remains expressly deferred;
  this review neither fixes nor certifies that path safe.

