remains pending at the time of this appendix.

The strict gate exposed an additional C1 constraint not established by the prior
source-only delta review: the pinned Slice 20 atomicity/nonblocking proofs must
remain runnable by default Cargo test, so a whole-target required-features gate
was inappropriate for that particular target. The correction removes that gate
and adds a self dev-dependency enabling the existing `test-hooks` feature for
engine development/test targets. The corresponding lockfile records only that
self edge. No production source, C1 checker, contract pin or proof body changes.
With workspace resolver 2, this dev-dependency does not propagate through an
ordinary external production dependency. The reviewer inspected the external
consumer sources and author-run compiler logs: normal Default/None compiles;
`TestEmbedderChoice`, `open_with_test_choice_for_test` and
`open_with_embedder_for_test` each fail as unavailable. The author-run default
Slice 20 execution log contains all eight passing tests, including both pinned
proofs. These are reviewed evidence, not independent reviewer executions.

The remaining two files reconcile the deliberate root-storage inventory with
owner-approved source removals. The documented field count and assertion change
44 to 41 by removing exactly `dense_disabled`, `dense_disabled_reason` and
`vector_equivalence_refusals`; the current lib.rs blob and test-module inventory
are recorded. Exact count, exact field membership, exact source-blob checks and
root-method restrictions remain. This is maintenance for approved API/state
removal, not relaxation of the invariant or regeneration of a golden oracle.
The author-run eight-test reconciliation log passes. No production provider
injection route or new runtime behavior is introduced by this delta.

## Verification coverage assessment

Under the standing `full-gate-run-frequency.md` instruction, completing the
original full run at c1ac plus final-source reruns of every failed suite and
targeted checks for the four-file correction is adequate; that correction alone
does not require repeating the whole gate. This assessment requires confirmation
that the original workspace Rust run already unified `test-hooks`, that all its
remaining stages finish, and that every additional failure is resolved and its
suite rerun. Default readiness execution, strict default/release compile matrix,
workspace Clippy and external normal-consumer closure specifically address the
dev-feature change. Broad production changes or materially different execution
feature coverage would require reassessment.

The final verification record must name source SHAs and retain the original
nonzero gate result. It may describe completed verification coverage with scoped
repairs; it must not claim that a whole `agent-verify` invocation passed at the
final HEAD when that invocation was not performed. The reviewer has not run or
certified those checks, and this policy assessment is not a pass result.

The reviewer subsequently inspected
`/tmp/fathomdb-provider-c1ac-engine-features.log`: the original workspace engine
test graph already enables `test-hooks` through the existing NAPI and Python
dev-dependencies. This confirms the feature-unification condition above; the
original full-run completion and remaining results are still root-owned.

